  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nAn upper level low has developed over the southeastern CONUS, while \na shortwave trough is ejecting northeast in vicinity of Nova Scotia \nand an additional trough digs into the northern Plains. A weak \nsouthern stream vort max will promote a retrograding stationary \nboundary in the Piedmont this afternoon. The vort max and front will \naid diurnal convective initiation within an environment of ~1500J/kg \nMLCAPE, 1.5-1.7 PWs, and weak steering flow. Latest 1 hr ffgs range \nfrom 1.4-1.8 inches in the foothills and East Tennessee mountains, \nincreasing to around 1.8-2.3 inches in southeast Tennessee and \nsouthwest North Carolina. While on the weaker side, latest \nmesoanalysis depicts a local increase in sfc-H85 convergence where \nstorms have notably strengthened over the last 30 minutes. Main \nthreats for this afternoon will be isolated strong to damaging winds \ngusts and localized flooding, but some of the tallest storms could \nexhibit small hail as well. \n\nUpper low is expected to remain in place across the region tomorrow \nafternoon, and slowly phase into mean flow aloft Thursday into \nFriday. However, the aforementioned troughing over the Northern \nPlains will be working into the Great Lakes region, maintaining weak \nupper level divergence across the southern Appalachians. Diurnal \nheating in a moist environment will ultimately promote continued \nchances of diurnal convection through at least the remainder of the \nweekend. With each day, isolated flooding could pose a threat with \nany slow moving storms that track over already saturated soils or \nwant to latch onto terrain features. While the potential for some \nvalley convection is possible, the greatest coverage is largely \nexpected in areas of enhanced orographic lift. \n\nAs we head into next week, the latest ECMWF depicts enhancement of \nthe low-level jet across the the northern Ohio Valley. This will \ncontinue the chance for daily showers and storms, but long range \ndeterministic models are not in agreement with the coverage. GFS \nis more bullish while the ECMWF is and the lower side. Latest NBM\nis bridging that gap with 40-50% PoPs. CIPS analogs keep the \nbetter chances for severe weather to our north and east in closer \nproximity to the strong synoptic forcing and enhanced shear.",
